content.text:
Here is what was going wrong and what we changed.

### Cause
`ProductPage` was an **async** server component that did `await getInventory()` at the top level. That call uses `connection()`, so nothing in the tree (including the **Premium Widget** heading) was sent until that work finished. Navigation felt slow because the whole segment waited on one request.

### Fix
1. **Sync page shell** — The default export is now synchronous and always renders the `<h1>Premium Widget</h1>` right away.
2. **`Suspense` around inventory** — Stock and price live in an async `Inventory` child inside `Suspense`, so only that part waits on `getInventory()`; the shell can stream first.
3. **Static `metadata`** — `export const metadata` sets the document title to **Premium Widget** without waiting on inventory.

The production build now labels `/product` as **Partial Prerender**: static HTML plus dynamically streamed content, which matches this layout.

```1:40:/vercel/sandbox/app/product/page.tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next'
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import { getInventory } from '@/lib/data'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'Premium Widget',
}

async function Inventory() {
  const inventory = await getInventory()

  return (
    <>
      <p>{inventory.count} in stock</p>
      <p>Price: ${inventory.price}</p>
    </>
  )
}

export default function ProductPage() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Premium Widget</h1>
      <Suspense
        fallback={
          <>
            <p>…</p>
            <p>…</p>
          </>
        }
      >
        <Inventory />
      </Suspense>
    </main>
  )
}
```

If you want a clearer loading state than the `…` placeholders (e.g. skeleton lines or “Loading inventory…”), say what you prefer and we can adjust the fallback.
